  "client_view_text": " The log is not stored in the same directory as the database file. Describe how the database administrator can use the log file to recover the database.\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a critical component of a database system that ensures consistency and recoverability of the database in the event of a crash or power failure. The WAL is a separate file from the database file, and it is maintained in a different directory. When a database transaction is committed, the WAL is updated before the database file is modified. This ensures that all transactions are valid and sequentially consistent, even in the event of a crash.\nIn the event of a crash, the database administrator can use the WAL file to recover the database. The WAL file contains a record of all transactions that were committed up to the point of the crash. By replaying the transactions in the log, the database can be recovered to a consistent state, ensuring that data integrity is maintained. The WAL file acts as a backup, allowing the database administrator to recover the database to a specific point in time, without having to perform a complete restore from backups.\nTo recover the database, the database administrator would follow these steps: 1) stop the database, 2) copy the current database file to a backup location, 3)"
